<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Venus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Venus is a Roman goddess whose functions encompass love, beauty, desire, sex, fertility, prosperity and victory. In Roman mythology she was the ancestor of the Roman people through her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a>, who survived the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> claimed her as his own ancestor, dedicating a Temple of Venus Genetrix (&quot;Venus the Mother&quot;) in 46 BCE. She was worshipped under many cult titles; Venus Erycina was a Punic statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a> captured from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eryx">Eryx</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> adapted the myths and imagery of her Greek counterpart <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>, and in the later classical tradition of the West she became the embodiment of love and sexuality, usually shown nude.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/venus-mythology.jpg" alt="Venus" width="330" height="511" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venus_Arles.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
